+All methods in this family operate within the differentiable volume rendering framework [G]. The core mechanism involves computing a depth estimate $\hat{D}(r)$ for a ray $r$ during the forward pass of the renderer and incorporating this value into the loss function or sampling strategy [G]. The predicted depth $\hat{D}(r)$ is computed as the expected depth along the ray, weighted by the transmittance and density at each sample point $t_i$:
 
 $$
-\hat{D}(r) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i t_i
+\hat{D}(r) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} t_i (1 - \exp(-\sigma_i \delta_i))
 $$
 
-where $w_i$ is the weight assigned to sample $i$, defined as:
-
-$$
-w_i = T_i (1 - \exp(-\sigma_i \delta_i))
-$$
-
-Here, $\sigma_i$ is the volume density, $\delta_i = t_{i+1} - t_i$ is the interval between adjacent samples, and $T_i = \exp\left(-\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \sigma_j \delta_j\right)$ is the accumulated transmittance up to sample $i$. This formulation ensures that depth contributions are proportional to the opacity of the surface encountered at that depth.
